# Cultural Landscapes Inventory: Gettysburg Landscape (NPS, 2000, rev. 2004), vegetation section, PDF pp. 55-57 (printed pages 17-19 of the inventory part).
# Text from the layout variant; punctuation restored in the quotes.
@ NPS-CLI-LANDSCAPE
55/17 | fields | Crops before 1863: wheat, oats, rye, corn, buckwheat, hay and pasture | - | pre-1863 | stated | Vegetation before 1863 was mainly cultivated and pastured fields; crop choice followed each farm's own needs and typical crops were wheat, oats, rye, corn, buckwheat and grassland harvested for hay and pasture | =Vegetation during the period prior to 1863 consisted primarily of cultivated and pastured fields. Crop selection was based on individual consumptive needs. Typical crops grown included wheat, oats, rye, corn, buckwheat, grassland harvested for hay and pasture. ~L
55/17 | woodlots | Woodlots before 1863: unimproved acres on steep, rocky, poorly drained ground, mostly along ridgelines | - | pre-1863 | stated | The 1860 census counted woodlots as unimproved acres, unimproved because of steep slopes, rocky ground, poor drainage or water courses; most woodlots followed ridgelines such as Warfield and Seminary Ridge, while Cemetery Ridge was mostly under cultivation | =Woodlots were another vegetation type. The 1860 census defined woodlots as unimproved acres. These areas were unimproved chiefly because of their steep slopes, rocky ground conditions, poor drainage or water courses. Most woodlots followed ridgelines such as Warfield and Seminary Ridge although Cemetery Ridge was mostly under cultivation. ~L
55/17 | woodlots | Woodlot use: fuel, building materials, game; understory open and cleared | - | pre-1863 | stated | Woodlots supplied fuel, building materials and game, and their understory was typically open and cleared because of these uses and the removal of deadwood to reduce fire hazard | =Woodlots provided fuel, building materials and game for the farm. Typically the understory was open and cleared as result of these uses (see photos) and for the removal of deadwood to reduce fire hazards. ~L
55/17 | orchards | Orchards on almost every active farm | - | pre-1863 | stated | Orchards were a typical complement to the cultivated fields of the mid-19th century landscape; almost all active farms had some sort of orchard and some farmers ran larger-scale operations | =Orchards were {{typical|a typical}} complement to the cultivated fields of the {{mid-l9th|mid-19th}} century agrarian landscape. Almost all of the active farms included some sort of orchard while some farmers participated in larger scale operations. ~L
55/17 | woodlots | Natural vegetation uncommon outside woodlots; land cleared for cultivation or pasture | - | pre-1863 | stated | Naturally vegetated corridors were uncommon outside woodlots on high or rocky ground and in drainage ways because most land had been cleared for cultivation or pasture | =Naturally vegetated corridors were uncommon outside of woodlots on high or rocky ground and in drainage ways as most of the land had been cleared for cultivation or pasture. ~L
55/17 | woodlots | Battle changed the vegetation little; vegetation gave cover and concealment | - | 1863-07 | stated | Apart from the trees felled for defence works on Culp's Hill, bullet-ridden trees and trampled fields, there were few actual changes to the vegetation during the battle, and vegetation provided cover and concealment for troop movement and battery positions | =bullet- and shot- ridden trees and trampled cultivated fields there were few actual changes to the vegetation on the battlefield. However vegetation played an important role in the battle. Vegetation provided cover and concealment for troop movement and battery position. ~L
55/17 | orchards | Sherfy's Peach Orchard and the Wheatfield became battlefield landmarks; Little Round Top west face lacked mature woods | bld-sherfy-farm,GZ-014 | 1863-07 | stated | Sherfy's Peach Orchard and the Wheatfield became battlefield landmarks: the first area marks Sickles' advancement and the second marks an area of intense fighting during the second day of battle | =Sherfys Peach Orchard and the Wheatfield became battlefield landmarks. The first area marks Sickles advancement and the second marks an area of intense fighting during the second day of battle ~L
55/17 | woodlots | Little Round Top's west face lacked mature woods | - | 1863-07 | stated | Little Round Top's lack of mature woods on its west face provided a superior position for defence | =Little Round Tops lack of mature woods on its west face provided superior position for defense ~L
55/17 | fields | Pattern of fields, orchards and woodlots unchanged in the GBMA period; few woodlots on gentle grades cleared | - | ~1863-1895 (GBMA period) | stated | During the GBMA period farming resumed and the pattern of fields, orchards and woodlots stayed basically unchanged, except that a few woodlots on gentle grades were cleared for cultivation | =During the GBMA period farming was reestablished and so the pattern of cultivated fields, orchards and woodlots remained basically unchanged. However few woodlots located on gentle grades were cleared for cultivation. ~L
56/18 | woodlots | Park commission replanted woodlots that had been cleared after the battle | - | 1895-1933 (War Department replanting) | stated | The War Department did some replanting of woodlots that had been cleared after the battle | =There was some replanting of woodlots that had been cleared after the battle. ~L
56/18 | fields | 1863 fields were small, 5-15 acres, of hay, corn, wheat, oats and rye | - | 1863-07 | stated | Fields at the time of the battle were small, 5 to 15 acres, of hay, corn, wheat, oats and rye; crops are now grown in 25 to 50 acre fields | =Crops typically grown today include hay, milo, corn, wheat, oats and barley grown in 25-50 acre fields as compared to the small scale 5-15 acre fields of hay, corn, wheat, oats and rye typically found during the time of battle. ~L
56/18 | orchards | No orchard trees from the battle survive today | - | 2000 condition (modern) | stated | The inventory says no orchard trees present during the battle exist today and that some orchards have been re-established by the NPS | =No orchard trees present {{turing|during}} the battle exist today. However some orchards have been reestablished by the National Park Service. ~L
